    "abstract" : "Modern System-on-Chip (SoC) architectures are becoming increasingly heterogeneous.The continuous proliferation and diversity of new accelerators increase the challenges faced both by hardware architects looking to design efficient SoCs, and software developers programming them. This thesis presents three contributions that help developers better understand the behavior of SoCs early in the design process. Our first contribution is AIO: An Abstraction for Performance Analysis Across Diverse Accelerator Architectures, which establishes a new metric for comparing performance across both contemporary and emerging accelerator architectures. AIO allows developers to compare application performance across diverse accelerators. As we show with AccMe, AIO can be used to build early-stage performance models, allowing developers to understand the broad performance trends of available accelerators when running a given program, without having to spend precious time porting code to each platform. Our second contribution is HILP: Accounting for Workload-Level Parallelism in System-on-Chip Design Space Exploration. Workload-level parallelism (WLP) is defined as the number of independent application phases concurrently executing on an SoC. HILP finds near-optimal schedules for workloads of multi-phase applications running on a given SoC, while respecting the limits of WLP that happen as a consequence of phase dependencies, in addition to limited global SoC resources such as memory bandwidth and power. In doing this, HILP shows the best way to share these resources across all SoC components. Modern SoCs may be used for many years, executing software that is rapidly changing. Our final contribution Neoscope: How Resilient Is My SoC to Workload Churn?, combines AIO and HILP to investigate what happens to performance when workloads change over the lifetime of a device. Neoscope provides mathematical definitions to quantify how workloads change, and discusses strategies for how to mitigate performance drop-off as this change occurs. These include not over-specializing small SoCs, and purposefully over-provisioning SoC resources if large input growth is expected."
  },
